#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# sin_integral

#% text
# `sin_integral(x)` gives the sine integral `Si(x)`, the integral of `sin(t)/t` for `t` from 0 to `x`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The definition is DLMF 6.2.9. `Si` is odd and entire, and tends to `pi/2` as `x` tends to `inf`.

#% item
# `sin_integral(0)` is 0. Machine numbers, balls and complex numbers give numerical values.

#% item
# `sin_integral` is listable.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
sin_integral(1.0)
#% output
#= 0.946083070367183

#% cell
sin_integral(0)
#% output
#= 0

#% cell
sin_integral(-2.0)
#% output
#= -1.6054129768026948

#% cell
n(sin_integral(pi), 30)
#% output
#= 1.851937051982466170361053370157991

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Large arguments approach `pi/2`:

#% cell
sin_integral(1000.0) - pi/2.0
#% output
#= -0.0005632048261252631

#% text
# A complex argument:

#% cell
sin_integral(1.0 + 1.0*im)
#% output
#= 1.1042226582355819 + 0.8824538050079177im

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# The Gibbs overshoot of a square wave's Fourier series, `Si(pi)/(pi/2)`:

#% cell
sin_integral(pi*1.0)/(pi/2)
#% output
#= 1.1789797444721675

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The derivative:

#% cell
d(sin_integral(x), x)
#% output
#= sin(x)/x

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `cos_integral`, `sinh_integral`, `exp_integral_ei`, `sinc`
